review_text:
“Un buon compromesso tra qualità, prezzo e location” Rispetto alla media degli alberghi di Manhattan, The Hotel at Times Square ha:
- camere piccole come la media (con un affaccio su un cortile interno con scala di emergenza, un muro di mattoni come unica visuale a 5 metri e pochissima luce);
- la colazione inclusa nel prezzo come quasi nessun hotel (il buffet non brilla per varietà e qualità, ma è sufficiente per cominciare la giornata);
- tariffe ragionevoli e competitive, specialmente in considerazione della media della zona;
- la location tra le migliori possibili, con metropolitana, Grand Central Station, Times Square, Rockefeller Centre e 5th Avenue, il tutto a due passi.
Nelle ore di punta si fa la coda per prendere l'ascensore, ma pulizia degli ambienti e cortesia del personale non mancano. Nel complesso offre un buon compromesso tra qualità, prezzo e location e se serve solamente come base per la notte è un scelta vantaggiosa.

“Nothing grand about the Grand Hyatt NYC” The good:
Friendly staff; speedy check-in and check-out; 24-hour gym;
The bad:
Noisy neighborhood, I've had better in Times Square; tiny bathrooms, I've seen better in some motels and lower classed hotels; no mini bar, never mind not having liquor but no water... The choice was to walk down the hallway and buy water via a vending machine... a vending machine!!! Not at all five star; room service menu was limited, and when I finally ordered something, it took 45 minutes to bring me the wrong item, it was replaced eventually but still....is room service really that busy; I was adjusting my body clock to the local time zone so i was sleeping in late, could not however because of the loud voices by the maid service, laughing and gossiping on the phone and with each other in the hallways;
I choose this hotel thinking it was the Grand Hyatt brand, but I've had better product quality in lower star hotels, service apartments and some motels

“Adequate for a short stay” We stayed at Hotel 41 for 4 nights, arriving December 25, 2005. The following are what we liked about our room:
1. the bed was comfortable and the sheets were clean;
2. the bathroom was clean;
3. there was a fridge in the room for water, etc.;
4. the bathroom had a dryer, make up mirror, Aveda products, and even a shower cap and Q-tips;
5. there was an iron;
6. the carpet was clean;
7. the staff were friendly;
8. the location was excellent.
We did not like the following:
1. the size of the room, which felt cramped and the bed was in an alcove with about a foot on each side to walk in;
2. there were no bed side tables;
3. there was a burn mark on the carpet that appeared to have been made by an iron;
4. there were spots on the bedspread;
5. the walls were dirty;
6. the water took 5 minutes to heat up. On the second day of our stay, there was no hot water all day. We were given a credit of $50.00 but would have preferred the hot water;
7. there was a smell of cigarette smoke coming from somewhere in the evenings;
8. the ironing board was one of those small ones that aren't very handy;
9. the cost of the room was too much for what you got - we paid $189.00 the first two nights and $269.00 the next two nights, not including any taxes;
l0. when we were given our bill, there was a space for a "tip". I have never seen this before on a hotel bill; and
11. the view was a brick wall.
Generally, we may have expected too much from this hotel, given some of the 4 star plus ratings. We did enjoy our stay in New York and at first we contemplated not sending in this review because our complaints are not major. However, since we use Tripadvisor all the time to check on rooms where we plan to go, we thought we had the obligation to let others know of our experience as well.

“An above average choice” Location: Good downtown location located in an office district attached to SwissHotel and next to Millennium Park. Within a 10-15 minute walk to downtown retail area ****;
Staff: Front desk staff professional and prompt. You have to request evening turn down service. Our room rate included breakfast and parking however the charges were on our bill. Front desk removed it ****;
Restaurant: We only had the breakfast buffet. It was an average basic buffet, definitely not worth $25.00 (even at hotel rates). Wait staff very friendly and efficient **1/2;
Rooms: We were upgraded to a suite with a great view. Room very clean and large. Furniture was dated and the room lacked any “wow” factor. Bed very comfortable. Room lighting was fair ****;
Wifi: Free for presidents club members not sure charge otherwise. ****;
Summary: Good hotel a step above average. Note parking (was included in our package) is expensive over $50.00/night. I noted that there is weekend parking available in the office building attached to the Fairmont for less than $15.00. There are better choices nearby for breakfast ****.

“Popularity of hotel is both blessing and curse” I stayed at the Residence Inn Chicago River North for a spring break trip with my family. Our trip included shopping, theater (Mary Poppins) and restaurant visits, along with a personal business trip to the Merchandise Mart.
The hotel is a nice, new facility with standard Residence Inn suites sized a bit smaller for the urban setting. It is in a great location for most Chicago Loop attractions, including State Street and the theater district. We left the car with valet parking and relied on our feet and the CTA (two el stations within three blocks). Valet parking is the only type offered and is EXPENSIVE - $44 per day (i.e., a two-night stay required three days of parking).
I am guessing that the great location and the new facility contributed to this being a popular hotel. The Residence Inn shares the building with a Springhill Suites. I don't know how many total rooms are in the 27-story building, but it seemed like a LOT of families stayed at the hotel, even on weekdays. While popularity is a good indicator for the hotel, the sheer number of people does stress the facilities and services:
- Free breakfast was packed and often under-supplied;
- The wait for valet parking was about 10-15 minutes at check-in;
- Elevators take some time for arrival;
- The pool is small and usually pretty full of young children
If all you need is a nice Residence Inn room and location, this is a great hotel. However, its overall value is diminished by the high cost of parking and the problems of handling a large number of guests.

“Schönes Hotel nur 5 Gehminuten vom Walk of Fame” Positives: sehr schöne und große Zimmer mit eigener kleiner Küchenecke mit Kühlschrank; sehr freundliches Personal an Rezeption und beim Parking; gutes kontinentales Frühstück; Kodak Theatre und Walk of Fame in 5 Gehminuten erreichbar; 
Negatives: Klimaanlage recht laut; Verkehrslärm vom 101-Zubringer nachts leider doch etwas störend – zumindest in unserem Zimmer mit direktem Blick auf die Straße
Fazit: Absolut empfehlenswert für alle, die in guter Hotelatmosphäre ganz nah am Walk of Fame wohnen wollen.

“clean, basic accomodation w/microwave & fridge” best western diamond elite member; paid $64+tax in december-2011 which is good rate for value received; appreciate upgrade to family suite with kitchen with utensils and separate bedroom and living room; ralphs and vons grocery stores, walgreens,, cvs near by; some restaurants near by but did not try them
clean, comfortable basic accommodation; chose for high traveladvisor rating and not disappointed except for minor irritants as detailed below; helpful, courteous staff; older property but well maintained; nice firm comfortable beds; plenty of clean towels
bulbs above sink outside bathroom burnt out but not replaced for two days despite request; 
at arrival, room very clean but housekeeping didn't clean interior of microwave where previous guest left spilled coffee & half full coffee cup but i didn't report instead cleaned myself; 
flat screen tv in bedroom goes blank for 30 seconds each time channel changed [most annoying] so instead watched CRT tv in living room; 
stayed 3 nights but housekeeping never left fresh paper coffee mugs or condiments packet until i left note asking for them; very clean room and well maintained property;
realize breakfast under reconstruction but general quality & variety of breakfast items poorer than comparable hotels; 
provided soap, shampoo and lotion but no conditioner
would definitely stay here again & would recommend to others

“Great for families, not-so-fab for couples/business travelers” As other reviews have noted, the Hyatt Hill Country offers a myriad of kid/teen-oriented activities. That, coupled with its close proximity to Sea World, make it a magnet for families. So, if you're considering it for a couple's getaway or a place to stay on business, I recommend that you aim for a time of year when school is in session: we were there for a Monday-Thursday stay the first week in June, and although I anticipated that a weekday stay would help work in favor of a couple's getaway, guests without children were in the distinct minority. Nothing against kids (I have a now-grown one myself), but the public areas (lobby, hallways, elevators, primary restaurant, and grounds just outside the hotel) are thus noisier & less serene.
We sprang for a suite, but unless you crave/need additional space, consider saving yourself the big $$ (we were unable to get the sweet deal on a suite snagged by previous reviewer). Contrary to our expectations, not one of the suites is -- according to the front desk clerk when we asked for another suite after first being assigned one right off the lobby -- an end unit. Rather, they are all at the "joints" of the wings. Although the suites do have a balcony, there is not even a partial wall or slatted railing dividing "your" balcony from your neighbors. Consequently,, ithe balconies end up being another common area offering not even a semblance of privacy... and serve as your neighbors' storage area for water toys, adjunct drying rack for beach towels, or supplemental play area for their children: great for them, but not for a couple hoping to have a quiet conversation & cup of coffee in the AM or a nightcap in the evening.
A couple of less-than-desirable quirks about the interior: 
* there is NO desk, so if you wish to use your laptop, you need to stand at the console housing the mini-fridge; the dining table & chairs are too far away from the modem connection to be a viable alternative to a desk;
* the TV's in both the bedroom and the living room are poorly situated for viewing from the bed or sofa.. best for just 1 person ensconced in the 1 armchair in each of those 2 rooms.; 
* there are no black-out drapes or other means of shielding the bedroom windows: the drapes do not pull all the way across the sheer undercurtains. We are early morning risers, so the sunlight streaming in at dawn didn't bother us, but others might not appreciate that. And, this "feature" too reduces one's privacy;
* for the price we paid, we were expecting, e.g., a more posh bathroom, less skimpy drawer space, and more than just 2 glasses in the suite (i.e., you won't mistake the Hill Country's suite for that of a Four Seasons)
On the up side:
* the lushly landscaped & spacious grounds are a welcome oasis from a day of sightseeing in the humid heat; 
* the adult-only pool & large "hot tub" spa set into a waterfall grotto are lovely & open from 6 AM 'til late at night; 
* we appreciated the free use of bicycles & the easy pool-side availability of beach towels; 
* the golf course gets rave reviews;
* the level of housekeeping & service by the staff was fine, albeit not especially noteworthy
* although you'll pay through the nose to use any of the business services almost always offered for free by other establishments in this (or even lower) price range, there is computer/printer by the concierge desk where you can print off boarding passes at no cost;
* given the distance to downtown San Antonio, the drive there is surprisingly quick & easy; highway congestion does not seem to be common, even during rush hours;
* it was great to be able to so quickly get out into the Hill Country. We had a fun day exploring there, the highlight of our trip imo.
Lastly, I thought the spa was fabulous. It's set off in a separate, very attractive building, somewhat off the beaten path, and has its own, tranquil outdoor pool & large whirlpool with courtyard/patio. The spa space is well thought-out; I received excellent service at prices not out-of-line for this type of facility; the staff was lovely and did not attempt to sell you any products. And yes, men are welcome there too -- plus, the spa offers a light, healthy food throughout the day. In short, couples, singles, and parents looking for a respite from being "on duty" will find this a delightful place to spend time.
I would return to the Hyatt Hill Country, primarily because of the spaciousness & physical beauty of its grounds. But we'll go back with reduced expectations, and not pay the extra money for a suite.

“Outstanding Facility” The hotel is located directly on Riverwalk on Market Street. The interior is beautiful; check-in was efficient and friendly. Rooms are well appointed and I absolutely love the bed and pillow at this Westin, I had two an incredible nights’ sleep. The bathroom was absolutely spotless, which is a huge bonus for any location. The HDTV was better than most and did not pixelate at all. The fitness center was excellent; 4 ellipticals, 4 cycles, and 8 treadmills, all first rate and in excellent condition. There are two benches for the free weights and one weight machine that allows for 20+ different exercises. The phical plant of this location is one of the nicest of the brand.
There are some process improvements that I would recommend to create even a better experience. 
• As a Platinum member I asked for an upgrade as part of my earned benefits. The front desk felt that a view of the river was a better option than a suite with a city view. I recommend that they ask the guest which they prefer since I would and did opt for the latter;
• The internet wireless service has three levels of speed that you choose when you first log in. Even with the highest speed I was required to re-start several times as pages would not load. Just be aware that the “handshake” can be lost often while on the wireless in the room. Waiting for someone from technical support to answer the phone was a long process and you need to be patient until someone picks up; 
• Coffee is available in the room via the mini machines but not in the lobby and the hotel does not have a lounge area. There is a Starbucks that opens at 6:30;
• The valet parking ($30 per night) is a broken process. It is the only location I know that takes your ticket every time you leave (strange that they focus on “green” yet the exchange is a waste of paper). Then when you return you have to sit and wait, then give your name and room number for a different ticket. I recommend that they do as the other 99.9% of the hotels do, just leave the same ticket for the entire stay. 
• The keys are electronic and during my stay two keys failed. The first occurred after I was rushed into the hotel during some police activity outside and I just wanted the safety of my room; the second occurred after exercising at 630 am, and I was glad the front desk clerk did not ask me for ID. 
• Zocca – Breakfast includes both a Continental and a Full Buffet plus a la carte from the menu. The Continental (only cold) buffet included boxed cereal (thankfully it included my favorite low-fat Granola) fruits, and breads. My only comment, which I conveyed to the MOD was that guests should be asked if they wanted to be seated inside or on the Riverwalk, outside. I was immediately shuffled to an interior table and likewise a party of 2 was initially shown a table inside and had to ask to be seated at one of the vacant tables overlooking the river.
Overall, this is a fantastic location and the physical property is gorgeous and well maintained, housekeeping was outstanding and the fitness center was top notch. There are a few processes that should be addressed. The General Manager did reach out to me and we had a productive conversation about the POV from a customer and how to make this hotel even better. On my next trip to San Antonio, this is definitely the place to stay.

“Convenient Location - Good hotel” Stayed 04/29/05 - 04/30/05.
Exactly what you would expect from a Holiday Inn Express:
Clean Rooms;
High Speed Internet;
Good cable selection;
Good front desk staff;
Breakfast;

“OK, but there are better Union Square choices” Having Hilton VIP status I elected to stay at the Hilton in Union Square for a recent business trip to San Francisco. I have previously stayed at other hotels in the area (e.g., Marriott, Prescott, Mark Hopkins) and found that the Hilton, while satisfactory, lags behind the others in several respects. These include: 
- older standard rooms in need of amenity upgrades (no wireless internet, no flat-panel TV, very small bathrooms);
- only one dining option on premises (although the breakfast buffet at the Urban Tavern is delicious and was comped during my stay);
- slightly closer to some of the more unsavory parts of the Theater District and Tenderloin than many of the other hotels
On the other hand, service staff was consistently friendly and the walk to Union Square takes less than 5 minutes. In sum, this is not a bad choice if you can get a good deal, but if you want a great Union Square hotel, I recommend either one of the top boutique places, like the Prescott, or one of the true luxury hotels such as the Fairmont.

“Overall very good, a few issues” Just spent a night at the relatively new Four Points Sheraton Columbus Airport. Got a good rate via Hotwire and that does have some bearing on my feeling.
Overall it was a very good experience, would certainly consider staying here again and would recommend it to others (especially at the rate we got via Hotwire).
I do have one *MAJOR* issue/complaint, however, the pool photo shown on their website? I'd like to know where the heck that pool is at the hotel. The hotel pool is a *postage stamp* size *indoor* pool - not a luxurious outdoor pool as "shown" on the website. As I look closer, it even appears that the pool on the website is a rendering. Maybe it was planned that way and/or will eventually be constructed, but it sure doesn't exist that way now.
Had two other *minor* issues, neither of which were really problems. First is that I had booked the room for 2 adults and 1 child, and they "gave" us a room with a single bed. I confess, I didn't call in advance to confirm a room with 2 beds, but that's because I never considered the possibility that they would give a booking for 3 people a single bed. There was absolutely no problem and the front desk quickly gave us a room with 2 beds, but if they hadn't had one available . . . (and I will always try to confirm a room with 2 beds when using Hotwire in the future).
The second was that the in-room coffee had 2 "decafs" and no regular coffees, hello? Again, not a problem and the front desk supplied "leaded" coffee without any questions upon request.
To recap my other observations:
Pro:
 Nice, new, hotel with nice lobby, etc;
 Staff was pleasant & professional, taking care of the minor glitches we encountered;
 Lobby bar had a good beer selection and prices weren't outrageous;
 Room was clean, and nicely decorated;
 Beds, linens, etc were nice;
 Nice shower (but see below).
Cons:
 Pool not as "advertised" on the website photos;
 Minor glitches encountered (see text of review);
 Bathroom was shower only, not a problem for me, and they might have rooms with tubs, but it's worth noting and might be a con for some travelers;
 Not much in walking distance (which I like in a hotel, but certainly don't expect with an "airport" hotel).
As much of what I talked about above issues/complaints, I don't want to give the impression I didn't like the hotel, I did like it. That said, but had I booked it expecting a nice outdoor pool, I would have suffered a *major* disappointment.

“From I-5 to bed in 5 minutes” I was up for a conference during the high season and was able to book this hotel for two nights of my six night stay in Seattle. 
My flight was late and didn't get to the hotel until around 2am. I wanted one thing -- to be lying in a comfortable bed.
I appreciated this hotel for several reasons - 
 its right off I5 - just a couple of turns and you are parked and checking in;
 the staff was excellent - always helpful and informative;
 the location is great - walking distance to Pike Place and all of that fun riff-raff;
 the lounge at the top of the hotel was quiet and comfortable with a great view (I'm a gold member so this probably doesn't apply to everyone);
 the queen bed was really comfortable;
 the room was quiet - your mileage may vary
I was in Seattle for a conference and booked some of my meetings in the lounge. That worked out perfectly, as it was private enough, there was coffee service and ample seating area.

“Total Package” This was my third visit to the Renaissance Hotel in D.C. This is a fairly large hotel that offers a good mix of benefits for the price. 
Positives
- quiet, despite renovations underway. Front desk was very accommodating in terms of choosing a room away from the renovation process; 
- generally a very positive service attitude among hotel employees;
- near many attractions, services, and transportation; 
- well organized for conferences;
- many different restaurant/deli/fast food options located in the nearby blocks. 
- Starbucks in the lobby. 
Negatives
- the poly-cotton sheets are bad enough, but this hotel also has poly-cotton towels. As part of the renovation, please consider upgrading to 100% cotton sheets and towels;
- bed was a bit soft for my taste; 
- curtains do not close fully to block out the light and it would be helpful to have some sort of clip to hold the curtains together; 
- uneven reception of cable channels;
- no refrigerator in room, although it looked like there was once a space for one in the armoire; 
- no free newspapers on the weekend;
- the carpet throughout the hotel is really hideous; it is working against the hotel's more modern image in the lobby; 
Despite those criticisms, I was happy with the hotel based on paying a special conference price. If I had paid a full rack rate for my room, I would probably expect more in terms of amenities. Still, the Renaissance will remain a top choice in D.C. for me at the right price point.

“Truly a gem with not a single negative element” Once again, Trip Advisor didn't let me down as all of the many positive reviews were dead on! I stayed here for 6 nights waiting for my apartment to be renovated and this is an amazing hotel for the following reasons:
1) Affordable for the Foggy Bottom area, especially on weekends, when the price drops significantly;
2). Rooms are like mini-apartments - with kitchen, silverware and cooking tools, living room with flat screen, comfortable bed with another TV, and thermostat settings in each room;
3). Great American breakfast every morning although variety is seriously lacking, there is plenty of breakfast food;
4). Free WiFi throughout the hotel with great connection;
5). Printed and faxed several documents for me without charging;
6). AMAZING LOCATION! 5 minute walk from Foggy Bottom metro and only 5-8 minute walk to the Watergate, where there's a Safeway and many restaurants; and
7). Great staff! They helped me extend my reservation several times as I waited for my apartment to finish.
This place tends to book fast so if you have time to plan I highly recommend this hotel!

“Hip and affordable at $98/night” Thanks to Hotwire, we were able to stay Saturday and Sunday at this boutique hotel for $98/night. We liked...
*friendliness of the staff, especially Michael;
*check in was very quick, and we were able to do so at 10 a.m. as our room was ready;
*Warholian George Washington paintings;
*minimalist, all creamy white and modern, room decor;
*spacious bathrooms with black granite and marble (but why no fan which made it a little steamy?);
*duvet-covered bed with the softest Egyptian cotton sheets;
*seating area with a lounge chair and ottoman plus an ergonomically designed chair at the black granite-topped desk;
*plenty of drawers and closet space to organize your belongings. In the closet there were also slipper socks, an umbrella and a safe, but we had quite a laugh imagining ourselves in the zebra and leopard print terry robes;
*complimentary New York Times;
*32-inch plasma-screen television. 
$40/night parking is high to us Ohioans, but my husband opted for the convenience rather than $19/day at nearby Union Station. Location was fine for walking to Newseum and National Gallery and for dinner at Matchbox on G Street. Used Metro from Union Station to get to Kennedy Center and took cab back that night.
Some reviewers have mentioned the homeless along E Street, but we didn't find anything intimidating in the situation. In fact, for breakfast before departing on Monday, we patronized New Course on E Street at the corner of Third. Made-to-order omelets for $3.75! Besides its affordablility, as a patron of New Course you become part of a community effort to provide culinary arts training for low income and homeless persons.
We'd be quite happy to return to Hotel George the next time we visit DC and be sure to plan a visit to the Capitol Building then.
